Subject: [PATCH] feat(dap): session badge on the status line, a quieter frame
 line, and gF
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Follow-up to the debug console after using it: it was not clear whether a
session was attached, stepping sometimes lost the current line, and there was
no quick way to look at a caller from a backtrace.

Stepping lost the current line
- Thread events made the thread they named the selected one. A program that
  starts or ends a thread while being stepped (hython has plenty) took the
  selection away from the stopped thread: stepping over `t.start()` left the
  source view where it was and dropped the line highlight, and the next `n`
  stepped the new thread into `genericpath.py`. Thread events no longer select
  a thread; a selected thread that exits is unselected.
- The first stop after attaching did not move to its line: the session was made
  active only after selecting the stopped thread, which is looked up in the
  active session. It is now made active first, and already on `initialized`,
  so the session is shown, and `Space G h` can pause it, before it first stops.
- `j` stops the selected thread again without resuming it, which used to be
  ignored; the view now follows it there.
- `n`, `s`, `o`, `c` mark the thread running when the request is sent rather
  than when the adapter answers. An answer handled after the next stop would
  otherwise clear the frame that stop selected. On a refused step the thread is
  selected again.

The current line
- The frame line was highlighted on the same line number of every buffer on
  screen, the console included; it is now drawn only in the frame's own file.
- `▶` in the gutter shows in every view of that file, not only a focused one,
  so the source view beside the console has it too.
- A `ui.highlight.frameline` background far from the editor's, like the dark
  red zed_onelight inherits, is toned down to a tint of it (`#e6ced0` for
  `#97202a` there), and drawn without the theme's foreground, so the code on
  the line keeps its syntax colours. Quiet colours and terminal palette colours
  are used as the theme has them.

Status line
- New `debug-session` element, by default right after `mode`: while a session
  runs it reads e.g. `DEBUG hython-bin pid 1234 · stopped` or
  `DEBUG port 5678 · running`, in `ui.statusline.debug`, else on the theme's
  `error` colour. A configured `left` list needs it added.
- What the session debugs is named when it starts: the process attached to by
  PID with its name, the program launched, or the debug server dialled.

gF: show a file:line beside
- New `peek_file_line`, on `gF`: shows the `file:line` under the cursor in the
  view beside -- from the console, the source view -- reusing it rather than
  splitting again, and keeps focus. It reads `file:line[:col]`, pdb's
  `file(line)` and a traceback's `File "file", line N`, preferring the one
  under the cursor, and skips files that do not exist. The line is marked with
  `ui.highlight` until that view's cursor leaves it. The next step, jump or
  frame change takes the source view back to the current frame.
- `Editor::dap_source_view` now also leaves out the focused view, so `gF` works
  from any buffer.

Docs: debugger.md (status line, current line, `gF`), editor.md (the element),
themes.md (`ui.statusline.debug`, the toned-down line), keymap.md and the
regenerated command list.

Tests: unit tests for the location parser, the tint and the session name; an
integration test for `gF` from the console. Checked by hand against debugpy in
tmux, before and after, including a script that starts a thread mid-step.
